---
// src/pages/bucknell/anop-102.astro  →  arizvi.com/bucknell/anop-102
// ANOP 102: the term week by week, 4 classes to try and a 5th to read. Not linked from the nav; sends noindex.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import CourseHeader from '../../components/CourseHeader.astro';
import CourseNav from '../../components/CourseNav.astro';
import PivotDemo from '../../components/PivotDemo.astro';
import BreakevenDemo from '../../components/BreakevenDemo.astro';
import LLMDemo from '../../components/LLMDemo.astro';
import RegressionDemo from '../../components/RegressionDemo.astro';
import LPFigure from '../../components/LPFigure.astro';
import { course } from '../../data/bucknell';

const c = course('anop-102');

// The term. Each week: the topic, what the class builds, and the class to try where there is one.
const weeks: { w: number; topic: string; build: string; try?: string }[] = [
  { w: 1, topic: 'A spreadsheet someone else can read', build: 'Inputs, formulas and outputs kept apart, named inputs, no numbers typed into formulas. A 1-tab model of a pricing decision.' },
  { w: 2, topic: 'Lookups and cleaning', build: 'Sorting, filtering, XLOOKUP and tidy tables. A sales export, cleaned.' },
  { w: 3, topic: 'Pivot tables and summaries', build: 'Averages, spread and totals by segment, and a 1-page "what happened" brief.', try: 'pivot' },
  { w: 4, topic: 'Charts that answer a question', build: 'Choosing the chart for the claim. 2 charts with a headline each.' },
  { w: 5, topic: 'Breakeven and cost-volume-profit', build: 'The model built from a case, with its assumptions in one place.' },
  { w: 6, topic: 'What-if analysis', build: 'Data Tables, scenarios and Goal Seek on the week 5 model.', try: 'breakeven' },
  { w: 7, topic: 'Review and midterm', build: 'A practice case, then the midterm.' },
  { w: 8, topic: 'Expected value', build: 'A forecast that comes with odds, and what to do with it. The class with the chatbot.', try: 'llm' },
  { w: 9, topic: 'Samples and confidence', build: 'How sure a sample lets you be, written up in plain words for a business claim.' },
  { w: 10, topic: 'Regression', build: 'A line through the data: fitting it, reading the slope and knowing when to doubt it.', try: 'regression' },
  { w: 11, topic: 'Linear optimisation', build: 'Formulating a product-mix decision and solving it with Solver.', try: 'overtime' },
  { w: 12, topic: 'Shadow prices and sensitivity', build: "The sensitivity report behind week 11's answer: which constraint to relax, and what it's worth." },
  { w: 13, topic: 'What-if with random draws', build: 'Profit under uncertain demand, run 1,000 times, as a range with a recommendation.' },
  { w: 14, topic: 'Team presentations', build: 'The decision memo and the model behind it.' },
];
---
<Base title="ANOP 102" description="ANOP 102, Spreadsheet Modeling & Data Analysis, as Ali Rizvi would teach it at Bucknell - the term week by week, with 4 classes you can try." noindex>
  <CourseHeader course={c} facts={[
    ['Format', '1 credit · 14 weeks · 2 classes of 75 minutes a week'],
    ['Tools', 'Excel. Laptops open while we build, closed for the debrief.'],
    ['Grading', 'Weekly labs 30% (lowest dropped) · midterm 20% · team project and memo 30% · final 15% · reflection 5%'],
    ['AI tools', 'Allowed for checking a formula or explaining an error. Hand in the chat with the lab. You stay responsible for every cell.'],
  ]}>
    <p>ANOP 102 is the first analytics course every Freeman student takes, and it runs in several sections a term. Where the department has a shared outline and exams, those come first. Below is a term as I'd plan it if asked, and 5 classes I'd bring to my sections whichever outline they run on. Every week adds a piece of the decision memo students hand in at the end.</p>
    <p class="practice"><span class="label">From practice</span>Resurge Therapeutics, a company in the portfolio of AMJ Capital, where I'm research lead, is running a first-in-human trial of an implant for an enlarged prostate: 20 patients at 4 sites, followed for 180 days, with the endpoint written down before the first patient. 20 is enough to see harm and too few to prove benefit, which is why safety is the primary endpoint and the symptom scores come second. Week 9 is that arithmetic: what a sample of 20 can and cannot tell you.</p>
  </CourseHeader>

  <Section label="A term, as I'd plan it" title="Week by week" id="term">
    <ul class="rows compact bk-wide term">
      {weeks.map((x) => (
        <li class="grid">
          <div class="label">Week {x.w}</div>
          <div class="body">
            <span class="title">{x.topic}</span>
            <p>{x.build}{x.try && <> <a href={`#${x.try}`}>{x.try === 'overtime' ? 'Read the class below.' : 'Try it below.'}</a></>}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Try a class · Week 3" title="Clean it before you count it" id="pivot">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>A coffee cart runs 2 locations on campus. Which one sold more last week? The till exported 12 rows and a pivot table adds them up by location. Both are below, as they came out.</p>
      <p>Count first. Then clean the export one problem at a time and watch the answer move.</p>
    </div></div>
    <PivotDemo />
  </Section>

  <Section label="Try a class · Week 6" title="How wrong can the forecast be?" id="breakeven">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>A taco truck for the summer: $600 a week for the truck, permit and insurance, $3 of ingredients a taco, $8 a taco. Last summer's truck sold about 140 a week. One cell says the plan makes $100 a week. A manager's next question is how sure that is.</p>
      <p>A two-way data table answers it: profit for every price and every volume at once. Then move the assumptions and watch the breakeven move.</p>
    </div></div>
    <BreakevenDemo />
  </Section>

  <Section label="Try a class · Week 8" title="An LLM gives you probabilities" id="llm">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>Students already ask chatbots for answers. This class shows them what comes back, and how to use it in a spreadsheet decision.</p>
      <p>An LLM doesn't look an answer up. It scores every word it could say next, turns the scores into probabilities, and picks one at random, weighted by those probabilities. Temperature sets how much it gambles: at 0 it always picks the favourite.</p>
      <p>Here a café asks one for tomorrow's croissant demand, then has to decide how many to bake. In the spreadsheet, each bake's expected profit is one SUMPRODUCT. In class the temperature slider waits until someone asks.</p>
    </div></div>
    <LLMDemo />
  </Section>

  <Section label="Try a class · Week 10" title="One weekend flips the slope" id="regression">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>A bike shop spent between $0 and $280 a week on Instagram ads for 12 weeks and logged its sales. Does the spend work? The shop keeps 40 cents of every sales dollar, so an ad pays for itself when $1 of ads brings in more than $2.50 of sales.</p>
      <p>Fit the line and read the slope. Then take out the one weekend that was different.</p>
    </div></div>
    <RegressionDemo />
  </Section>

  <Section label="Sample class · Week 11" title="Which overtime is worth paying for?" id="overtime">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>A 75-minute class. A small shop builds 2 bikes. A Commuter earns $120 over its materials and takes 3 hours of assembly and 1 of painting. A Trail earns $180 and takes 4 and 3. The crew is paid for 240 assembly hours and 120 painting hours a week whatever gets built, and only 30 Trails sell.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Guess first.</strong> Which bike do you build more of? The Trail earns more per bike, so it's the tempting answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Pairs, then the room.</strong> Pairs write the decision variables, objective and constraints on paper, then build and solve the model.</li>
        <li><strong>The real decision.</strong> The owner can buy overtime: an assembler at $30 an hour or a painter at $25. Which is worth it?</li>
        <li><strong>Exit ticket.</strong> Tell the owner what to do in 2 sentences, and what would change your answer.</li>
      </ol>
    </div></div>
    <div class="row-fig"><LPFigure /></div>
  </Section>

  <CourseNav current={c.slug} />
</Base>
